Task execution and evaluation. We use Lagent , Leval , and Lopt to denote the language models
serving as the agent executor, task evaluator, and workflow optimizer, respectively. In our homoge-
neous setting, Lagent = Leval = Lopt . For two outputs y1 and y2 and an evaluation prompt xeval ,
we write y1 ≻ y2 if Leval prefers y1 over y2 . We denote the corresponding rationale (or feedback)
sampled from Leval as d ∼ Leval (y1 , y2 ; xeval ).
Goal definition. Let L(g) denote the language model in MASS after g = 0, · · · , R generations of
RSI, our goal is to increase the probability of y (g) ≻ y (<g) from the perspective of an external strong
language model Lext (for the purpose of fair comparisons), where (τ (g) , y (g) ) ∼ A(xt , L(g) ).

3   M ETHOD
Overview. MASS alternates between multi-agent workflow optimization (Algorithms 2) and
model fine-tuning that internalizes the workflows (Algorithms 3). At generation g, the current model
L(g) serves as the task-solving agent, workflow optimizer, and evaluator. Algorithm 2 first produces
task-specific retained workflows using fixed model weights. Algorithm 3 then generates and self-
selects trajectories under these workflows and fine-tunes the same model to obtain L(g+1) . The
updated model assumes all three roles in the next generation. Thus, MASS forms a recursive loop in
which the current model first improves how copies of itself coordinate, then learns from the result-
ing coordinated trajectories. The next generation repeats the same process with the updated weights.
After R iterations, we evaluate L(g) on Ttest with Lext for all g and report the test score.

Algorithm 1: Recursive self-improvement with MASS
Input: training tasks Ttrain ; test tasks Ttest ; coding agent A; initial trainable language model
        L(0) ; number of MASS cycles R
for g = 0, 1, . . . , R − 1 do
    Set Lagent = Leval = Lopt = L(g) ;
                                     ∗,(g)
    Obtain optimal workflows {wt }t∈Ttrain using Algorithm 2;
                                                               ∗,(g)
    Obtain L(g+1) by applying Algorithm 3 to L(g) and {wt }t∈Ttrain ;
end
return L(R) ;

Algorithm 2. Multi-agent workflow optimization. Defining multi-agent workflow in the prompt
is demonstrated to be an effective way for LLMs to follow the organization instruction and act
accordingly (Nielsen et al., 2026; Tang et al., 2026). Following this, we define a workflow as a
textual specification of an orchestrator–subagent architecture that is appended to the task prompt
and guides how the orchestrator delegates work, exchanges information, and invokes subagents.
                                                                                        mt
Specifically, for task t, we represent the workflow as wt := {ht , (rt,j , ut,j , ct,j )j=1 }, where mt
is the number of subagents. Here, rt,j specifies subagent j’s role, ut,j its instructions, and ct,j its
output contract. The contract specifies what information the subagent returns to the orchestrator,
while ht specifies the order of subagent calls. We concatenate wt to the task prompt xt , and execute
the coding agent as A(xt ⊕ wt ; Lagent ). The orchestrator and all subagents share the same Lagent . A
